  "live_records": {
    "q-71206": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71206",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $H$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},5) & =\\frac{6}{5-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 2 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
