    "q-71193": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71193",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $T(2,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $T(2,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =x-{y}^{2} \\\\ x & =1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $T(2,1)$ is the parabola $x=1+{y}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
