    "q-128449": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128449",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+2}{1-3y}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =\\frac{3(0)+2}{1-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3x+2}{1-3y} & =-1 \\\\ 3x+2 & =-(1-3y) \\\\ 3x+2 & =3y-1 \\\\ 3y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the line $y=x+1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
