    "q-71219": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71219",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $F$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{7},1) & =\\frac{12(1)}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-{1}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 6y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+9 & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the hyperbola $-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
