    "q-129057": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129057",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola",
      "worked_solution": "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =c({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(1-c){y}^{2} & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse. So, I is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curves are $x=\\pm 1,$ which consists of two lines. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=3,$ which is a hyperbola. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
